// BUG-007 (GAP-Z008 regression): Numeric-constrained parameters default to Float,
// causing recursive calls to fail type checking when the accumulated arithmetic
// result (e.g. `a % b` where a::Float, b::Int) is passed to an Int-typed parameter.
//
// Root cause: zymbol-semantic/src/type_check.rs types_compatible_static() has no
// (Float, Int) arm — falls through to _ => false — so a % b (typed Float because
// Numeric.to_type() = Float) is rejected when gcd's second param is Exact(Int).
//
// Reproduction: define a recursive function whose parameter has only Numeric
// constraints (no Int evidence), then recursively pass arithmetic over that param.
//
// Expected: all three functions run and produce correct output
// Actual:   "argument 2 has type Float, but function 'gcd' expects Int" (before fix)
//
// Fix: added (ZymbolType::Float, ZymbolType::Int) => true to types_compatible_static()
//      in crates/zymbol-semantic/src/type_check.rs — bidirectional numeric compatibility.

// Caso 1 — GCD (algoritmo de Euclides): param `a` sin evidencia de Int → Numeric → Float
// La llamada recursiva gcd(b, a % b) fallaba con el checker
gcd(a, b) {
    ? b == 0 { <~ a }
    _ { <~ gcd(b, a % b) }
}
>> gcd(48, 18) ¶
>> gcd(100, 35) ¶
>> gcd(17, 19) ¶

// Caso 2 — función aritmética pura que recibe tanto Int como Float
multiplicar(a, b) { <~ a * b }
>> multiplicar(3, 4) ¶
>> multiplicar(3.0, 4.0) ¶

// Caso 3 — potencia recursiva con param sin evidencia de Int
pot(base, exp) {
    ? exp == 0 { <~ 1 }
    _ { <~ base * pot(base, exp - 1) }
}
>> pot(2, 10) ¶
